  "status": "published",
  "title": "Soil Moisture Monitor",
  "summary": "An ESP32 sensor that texts you when the beds are dry. A gentle first step into microcontrollers.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 4,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Install the Arduino IDE and the ESP32 board package, plug in the ESP32 and upload the Blink example to confirm the board and USB driver work.",
    "Wire one capacitive sensor with jumper wires: VCC to 3.3 V, GND to GND, AOUT to GPIO 34. Use only ADC1 pins (GPIO 32-39); ADC2 pins stop reading once Wi-Fi is on.",
    "Calibrate: print the raw reading with the probe in dry air and again in a glass of water, and set the dry threshold about a third of the way from the wet value to the dry one.",
    "Add Wi-Fi and a notification webhook (ntfy.sh for a free phone push, or IFTTT or Twilio for a real SMS). Read the sensor, send one message when it crosses the threshold, keep an 'already alerted' flag in RTC memory, then deep-sleep for 30 minutes.",
    "Move the circuit from jumper wires to a soldered perfboard, drill the enclosure with the step bit and bring the sensor cable and USB lead in through cable glands.",
    "Seal the sensor's exposed top-edge electronics with silicone or heat-shrink (the board is not waterproof above the marked line), push it into the bed up to the line, and power it from an outdoor-rated outlet."
  ],
